18
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:47,020
This is the official architecture diagram provided by Anthropic.

19
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:50,140
Harness is built around an agent loop that is code-centric.

20
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:51,820
responsible for tool planning,

21
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:53,020
invoking the context,

22
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:55,680
managing error recovery and iterative results.

23
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:59,280
Tools are the interface that allows the agent to connect to external capabilities.

24
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:01,320
This includes reading and writing files, as well as executing code.

25
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:03,020
perform web access,

26
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:05,730
as well as calls to MCP servers and business systems.

27
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:09,440
This provides an isolated environment for tool execution.

28
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:11,960
This allows the agent to execute commands securely.

29
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:14,479
processing files and accessing controlled resources.

30
00:01:14,479 --> 00:01:17,739
A session holds the state for a long-running task.

31
00:01:17,739 --> 00:01:19,200
Events and Outputs

32
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,200
enabling tasks to run asynchronously.

33
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,500
Resume from breakpoints and track progress continuously.

34
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:24,820
Orchestration

35
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:27,200
responsible for the model tool sandbox,

36
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:30,400
orchestrating memory permissions and the event stream.

37
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,760
to create a managed service.

38
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:32,660
observable,

39
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:35,780
and an auditable production runtime layer.

40
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:38,330
But with managed agents,

41
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:41,810
And the biggest problem stems precisely from its closed, hosted nature.

42
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:42,490
enterprise

43
00:01:42,490 --> 00:01:45,340
handing over the most critical control to an external platform.

44
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:46,500
The first problem is...

45
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,289
Harness becomes a black box.

46
00:01:48,289 --> 00:01:51,469
If this layer is hidden behind the Agent API,

47
00:01:51,469 --> 00:01:53,409
all the enterprise gets is the final output.

48
00:01:53,409 --> 00:01:56,010
making it difficult to inspect and modify the agent itself.

49
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:57,110
The second issue...

50
00:01:57,110 --> 00:01:58,310
Memory Lock-in.

51
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:00,390
And this lock-in isn't just about the model.

52
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:01,440
And then there's memory.

53
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:02,120
Memory.

54
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:03,220
This includes conversation history,

55
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:04,400
long-term memory, and so on.

56
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,320
But the problem is, businesses can't manage or utilize this memory.

57
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:08,500
The third issue is...

58
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:10,220
Limited model selection.

59
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:13,140
Managed agents can only use twelve models.

60
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:14,360
The fourth issue is observability.

61
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,089
Observability boundaries.

62
00:02:16,089 --> 00:02:19,009
Enterprises can only see the events that the platform is willing to expose.

63
00:02:19,009 --> 00:02:23,460
This makes it difficult for them to conduct their own audits, reviews, evaluations, and compliance checks.

64
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:27,780
The Expert platform, on the other hand, is an agent natively built for the enterprise.

65
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,840
Building the platform

66
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:31,000
The platform is deployed within the enterprise's own environment.

67
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:31,540
Going forward,

68
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:34,829
All resources can be brought within the enterprise's own control perimeter.

69
00:02:34,829 --> 00:02:36,769
As for the black box issue with Harness,

70
00:02:36,769 --> 00:02:38,889
On the Expert platform, enterprises can

71
00:02:38,889 --> 00:02:40,949
tailored to their specific business needs.

72
00:02:40,949 --> 00:02:43,269
to build and manage their own agents.

73
00:02:43,269 --> 00:02:45,069
Now, regarding the issue of memory lock-in,

74
00:02:45,069 --> 00:02:47,429
The Expert platform will take memory information,

75
00:02:47,429 --> 00:02:48,309
knowledge base,

76
00:02:48,309 --> 00:02:51,540
and resources like log information are all persistently stored.

77
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:54,300
These resources reside within the enterprise's own deployment environment.

78
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:56,850
This allows enterprises to review and manage them at any time.

79
00:02:56,850 --> 00:02:58,950
As for the issue of limited model selection,

80
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:01,710
The Expert platform supports the selection of different models.

81
00:03:01,710 --> 00:03:04,070
Enterprises can fully tailor their choices based on the complexity of their business.

82
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:06,100
and select the right model provider.

83
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:07,760
Regarding observability,

84
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:10,220
The Expert platform imposes no such restrictions.

85
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:12,400
Enterprises can choose based on their own needs.

86
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:14,960
audit or review historical log information.

87
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:17,520
You can also track the agent's execution process.

88
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:19,300
and evaluate the agent's performance.

89
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:22,980
So, the Expert platform doesn't just provide the Harness framework,

90
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:27,360
but also empowers enterprises to manage this powerful capability securely on their own.

91
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,730
This is the Harness engineering architecture within the Expert platform.

92
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:32,870
At the heart of this architecture are the Expert agents.

93
00:03:32,870 --> 00:03:35,930
Internally, each Expert consists of two core layers of capabilities.

94
00:03:35,930 --> 00:03:39,910
The LOLM and Harness large models are responsible for reasoning and action.

95
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:41,570
Harness is in charge of control.

96
00:03:41,570 --> 00:03:44,260
At the execution layer, it provides a sandboxing mechanism.

97
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:47,160
The sandbox provides an isolated file system and environment.

98
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:49,440
This allows the expert to read and write files securely.

99
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:51,680
execute commands and process task outputs.

100
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:55,459
The Capability Extension Layer provides tools and MCPs.

101
00:03:55,459 --> 00:03:56,799
They take external systems,

102
00:03:56,799 --> 00:03:59,879
They encapsulate business interfaces and professional tools into standardized capabilities,

103
00:03:59,879 --> 00:04:01,610
for the expert agents to call upon as needed.

104
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:04,590
The Runtime Enhancement Layer provides various middleware.

105
00:04:04,590 --> 00:04:05,550
short-term memory,

106
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:06,490
long-term memory,

107
00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:07,910
capabilities like its skill mechanism,

108
00:04:07,910 --> 00:04:10,650
All of which enter the expert's runtime process through middleware.

109
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:12,390
So, this Harness architecture...

110
00:04:12,390 --> 00:04:15,130
providing businesses with an entire infrastructure.

111
00:04:15,130 --> 00:04:19,450
This empowers enterprises to easily build, deploy, and manage their agents.

112
00:04:19,978 --> 00:04:22,699
Next, let's dive into the sandboxing mechanism of the Expert platform.

113
00:04:22,699 --> 00:04:24,339
The first layer is the agent.

114
00:04:24,339 --> 00:04:26,839
The agent itself doesn't directly interact with Docker.

115
00:04:26,839 --> 00:04:29,190
Instead, it enters the sandbox through two types of tools.

116
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:31,490
file tools and a sandbox shell.

117
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:32,790
The second layer is Docker.

118
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:34,100
The sandbox back then...

119
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,200
This provides a unified interface for these capabilities.

120
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:37,660
This includes file read and write operations,

121
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:39,520
command execution and service proxying.

122
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,920
The third layer is the Docker sandbox provider.

123
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:43,860
It manages Docker resources.

124
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:45,830
It's responsible for creating and reusing containers,

125
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:49,550
as well as managing images, network volumes, and the container lifecycle.

126
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:53,690
So, the core of the Sparse sandboxing mechanism is its three-layer isolation for the agent.

127
00:04:53,690 --> 00:04:55,310
It only calls standard tools.

128
00:04:55,310 --> 00:04:55,990
Middleware

129
00:04:55,990 --> 00:04:57,450
for unified interception and routing.

130
00:04:57,450 --> 00:04:59,970
The provider centrally manages the Docker runtime environment.

131
00:04:59,970 --> 00:05:03,840
This prevents the agent from directly accessing the underlying Docker resources.

132
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:04,940
And there are a few other details.

133
00:05:04,940 --> 00:05:09,170
First, the agents on the COSPORT platform don't run inside a sandbox environment.

134
00:05:09,170 --> 00:05:10,310
After the two interfaces are connected,

135
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:11,630
Even if the sandbox encounters an error,

136
00:05:11,630 --> 00:05:13,510
it won't affect the agent itself.

137
00:05:13,510 --> 00:05:16,010
The sandbox mechanism of the 2spurt platform.

138
00:05:16,010 --> 00:05:19,450
The file paths of the sandbox and the host machine are mapped to each other.

139
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:20,750
Even if the sandbox encounters an exception,

140
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:22,270
all files will still be there.

141
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:24,490
and it can resume normal operation after a restart.

142
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:28,350
Third, the file paths are isolated on a multi-tenant basis.

143
00:05:28,350 --> 00:05:30,650
This ensures security between different departments within the enterprise,

144
00:05:30,650 --> 00:05:33,490
and secure isolation between different employees.

145
00:05:34,509 --> 00:05:38,120
And that concludes our overview of the sandboxing mechanism in the Expert platform.

